title: The sync database
tags: Sync
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: A pair's links and snapshots, in one small key-value store under the host's internal/ — not metarecords, not in the event log, one copy per pair.

!! Where

`.metafolder/internal/sync-<a>-<b>/`, an LMDB store, where `<a>` and `<b>` are the
two repository uuids in ''canonical order'' — the smaller uuid is A. That order
names the store, the `a`/`b` roles of the HTTP API and of the plan, and the
perspective a snapshot's values are stored in; the order the user names the
repositories in never matters.

It sits in one repository's `internal/` — the ''host'': canonical A unless
`mf sync plan --host` (or `mf sync link --host`) chose the other when the store was
created. There is one copy: every change is one transaction, so the link state is
always consistent, and losing the store loses only the pair's sync state — the next
sync starts over as a first sync. A pair is found by looking in both repositories'
`internal/`; in both is a `409` until the stale one is deleted (the daemon never
merges two), in neither is an empty pair. Its `repositories` table names the pair,
checked on every opening (`409` on a mismatch — a store copied from another pair),
and the host, updated when the directory was moved by hand.

The daemon keeps a store open once a request has opened it — two requests on one pair
share it, LMDB allowing one opening of a file per process — and closes it when the host
repository is let go (unloaded, or lost when its root moved). Kept for the life of the
process, a store that moved with its root was opened a second time at the new place
once the repository was loaded there.

!! What

|!Table |!Key |!Value |
|`version` |`format_version` |the store's format (`3`), a big-endian u32 |
|`repositories` |`a`, `b`, `host` |the uuid of the repository in that role (16 bytes) |
|`links` |link uuid |`record_a` · `record_b` (16 bytes each) · `version_a` · `version_b` (a byte `0` when absent, else `1` and 8 big-endian bytes) |
|`links_by_record_a`, `links_by_record_b` |record |its link — one link per record, on each side |
|`snapshots` |link uuid |the link's snapshot, every entry in one value |

The versions are those of the two records at the last commit, absent before the
first ([[Change detection in sync]]). A snapshot entry is a field name and a value,
either ''common'' — what both sides held, in A's perspective, with a B-perspective
uuid of a `ref` target or a `tree_ref` parent — or ''per side'' (`a` or `b`), what
one side held alone: its content and mode stamps. A commit replaces a link's
versions and snapshot together; deleting a link deletes its snapshot. A snapshot is
read and written whole, so it is one value, in the store's compact encoding
([[Value encoding in the store]]):

```
snapshot = varint count · { entry }
entry    = varint len · name · flags · type · value [· uuid_b]
flags    = side (0 common, 1 a, 2 b) | 4 when uuid_b follows
```

An empty snapshot is an absent one. The entries are not grouped by field as in a
record: the side and the B-perspective uuid differ from value to value.

!! Access

The daemon opens a pair's store when a primitive first needs it and keeps it open
for the process — LMDB allows one opening per process, and two requests on one pair
may run at once. Writes serialise on LMDB's write lock; nothing else orders the
calls on a pair: concurrency between a sync and other writers is what
`expected_version` is for ([[Running a sync]]). A second daemon cannot touch the pair:
both repositories must be loaded, and each is locked by the daemon that holds it.

Format 2 (until November 2026) had a `meta` table of text values in place of
`version` and `repositories`, `by_a` and `by_b` for the two indexes, and `snaps`, one
key per snapshot entry (link uuid · index) holding it in JSON, in the column form
([[The column form of a value]]). Such a store is converted when it is opened, in one
transaction, its links, versions and snapshots kept, and its tables removed; a store
of a newer format is refused ([[Persisted format changes]]).

Until September 2026 the store was a SQLite file, `sync-<a>-<b>.sqlite`; none
existed outside the tests, so nothing converts one. A snapshot from before
positions were snapshotted as `tree_ref` values holds `mfr_path` as a string: that
entry is ignored, and the two paths compared afresh at the next sync.
